1
00:00:00,304 --> 00:00:04,746
Today, almost every person on
Earth lives inside a country.

2
00:00:04,766 --> 00:00:09,119
The United Nations alone has a
hundred and ninety three members.

3
00:00:09,139 --> 00:00:10,980
Each one has someone in charge.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,780
Presidents. Prime ministers.

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,802
Kings and queens. There
are police on the streets.

6
00:00:15,822 --> 00:00:17,803
Courts. Prisons. Taxes.

7
00:00:17,823 --> 00:00:20,290
And there are laws for almost everything.

8
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:21,978
How fast you can drive.

9
00:00:21,998 --> 00:00:23,203
Where you can park.

10
00:00:23,223 --> 00:00:24,681
What you can build.

11
00:00:24,701 --> 00:00:28,259
It feels like humans have always
needed someone in charge.

12
00:00:28,279 --> 00:00:30,806
Now take time back fifty thousand years.

13
00:00:30,826 --> 00:00:34,072
A band of about thirty people
sets up camp for the night.

14
00:00:34,092 --> 00:00:35,684
No president. No police.

15
00:00:35,704 --> 00:00:37,818
No written laws. No prison.

16
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:40,018
So who decides where to go tomorrow?

17
00:00:40,038 --> 00:00:41,518
Who settles a fight?

18
00:00:41,538 --> 00:00:45,652
And what stops the strongest man
from taking whatever he wants?

19
00:00:45,672 --> 00:00:48,702
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

20
00:00:48,722 --> 00:00:52,814
That's easy. The biggest guy with
the biggest club was the boss.

21
00:00:52,834 --> 00:00:55,040
Everyone else just did what he said.

22
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:56,716
At least, that's what we assume.

23
00:00:56,736 --> 00:01:00,666
But when anthropologists lived with
hunter-gatherers, they found something

24
00:01:00,686 --> 00:01:05,916
surprising. A hunter who brings home
a huge animal gets mocked for it.

25
00:01:05,936 --> 00:01:11,067
A man who acts like a boss gets
laughed at, ignored, or left behind.

26
00:01:11,087 --> 00:01:16,156
And the way they kept the peace may be
one of the oldest human inventions of all.

27
00:01:16,176 --> 00:01:19,483
To see it, we have to start
with our closest cousins.

28
00:01:19,503 --> 00:01:21,815
Chimpanzees definitely have bosses.

29
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:25,586
Every group has an alpha male,
at the top of a strict ranking.

30
00:01:25,606 --> 00:01:28,729
Others bow to him, and greet
him with little grunts.

31
00:01:28,749 --> 00:01:31,457
But becoming the boss
isn't just about muscle.

32
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:36,421
In the nineteen seventies, the scientist
Frans de Waal watched a chimp colony at

33
00:01:36,441 --> 00:01:38,090
a zoo in the Netherlands.

34
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:41,646
He saw that becoming the boss
took friends, not just muscle.

35
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:46,546
The old alpha, Yeroen, lost his
place to a stronger male called Luit.

36
00:01:46,566 --> 00:01:49,505
So Yeroen teamed up with
a young male, Nikkie.

37
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:54,443
Together, they made Nikkie the new alpha,
while old Yeroen kept a lot of real

38
00:01:54,463 --> 00:01:56,779
influence behind the scenes.

39
00:01:56,799 --> 00:01:59,136
He called his book Chimpanzee Politics.

40
00:01:59,156 --> 00:02:03,851
And at Gombe in Tanzania, in nineteen
sixty four, Jane Goodall watched a

41
00:02:03,871 --> 00:02:07,289
low-ranking chimp called
Mike take over his group.

42
00:02:07,309 --> 00:02:09,376
Mike didn't have the biggest muscles.

43
00:02:09,396 --> 00:02:12,551
He'd even lost two of
his big canine teeth.

44
00:02:12,571 --> 00:02:16,058
He had empty kerosene
cans, from Goodall 's camp.

45
00:02:16,078 --> 00:02:20,633
He charged at the top males, kicking and
banging the cans, making a terrifying

46
00:02:20,653 --> 00:02:22,242
racket. They fled.

47
00:02:22,262 --> 00:02:24,080
Then they came back, and groomed him.

48
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:27,153
Within about four months,
Mike was the alpha.

49
00:02:27,173 --> 00:02:32,178
Later researchers at Gombe noticed
that alphas have different styles.

50
00:02:32,198 --> 00:02:36,403
The smallest alpha they studied
spent the most time grooming others.

51
00:02:36,423 --> 00:02:38,515
Friends instead of muscle.

52
00:02:38,535 --> 00:02:42,454
Our other close cousins, bonobos,
do things differently again.

53
00:02:42,474 --> 00:02:47,640
When a male bonobo bullies a female,
other females often team up and chase him

54
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:53,315
away. In one long study, most of the
females' alliances were aimed at males.

55
00:02:53,335 --> 00:02:56,581
And older females back
up the younger ones.

56
00:02:56,601 --> 00:03:00,506
So our ancestors probably
started out with bosses too.

57
00:03:00,526 --> 00:03:05,151
But somewhere along the way,
humans did something very unusual.

58
00:03:05,171 --> 00:03:07,379
Life in a hunter-gatherer band was small.

59
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:12,517
A typical camp held a few dozen people,
often somewhere between twenty five and

60
00:03:12,537 --> 00:03:14,865
fifty. And they weren't all family.

61
00:03:14,885 --> 00:03:19,350
In twenty eleven, researchers looked
at who lived together in thirty two

62
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:21,285
hunter-gatherer societies.

63
00:03:21,305 --> 00:03:24,461
Most people in a band weren't
close blood relatives.

64
00:03:24,481 --> 00:03:27,380
They were friends, in-laws,
and friends of friends.

65
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:32,280
Grown-up brothers and sisters often lived
together, and both men and women could

66
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,949
move between groups.

67
00:03:33,969 --> 00:03:35,435
So who was in charge?

68
00:03:35,455 --> 00:03:38,710
Among the Hadza of Tanzania,
the answer is nobody.

69
00:03:38,730 --> 00:03:40,187
There are no chiefs.

70
00:03:40,207 --> 00:03:42,950
Some people are respected,
and listened to.

71
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:45,253
But nobody can give orders.

72
00:03:45,273 --> 00:03:49,153
Decisions get made by talking,
often for a long time.

73
00:03:49,173 --> 00:03:50,828
And if you don't like a decision?

74
00:03:50,848 --> 00:03:52,514
You can just leave.

75
00:03:52,534 --> 00:03:57,501
Among the Ju'hoansi, certain families were
known as the owners of a waterhole and

76
00:03:57,521 --> 00:03:58,989
the land around it.

77
00:03:59,009 --> 00:04:01,764
But that made them hosts, not bosses.

78
00:04:01,784 --> 00:04:03,831
Nobody could give orders.

79
00:04:03,851 --> 00:04:07,491
Hadza camps change all the
time, as people move in and out.

80
00:04:07,511 --> 00:04:12,593
If two people really can't get along,
one of them simply moves to another camp.

81
00:04:12,613 --> 00:04:14,731
Think about how powerful that is.

82
00:04:14,751 --> 00:04:17,695
A boss is only a boss if people stay.

83
00:04:17,715 --> 00:04:20,637
But hunter-gatherers
didn't just avoid bosses.

84
00:04:20,657 --> 00:04:22,506
They actively stopped them.

85
00:04:22,526 --> 00:04:27,655
The anthropologist Christopher Boehm
studied reports from forty eight societies.

86
00:04:27,675 --> 00:04:33,055
He found that, again and again, ordinary
people teamed up to keep any one person

87
00:04:33,075 --> 00:04:34,933
from getting too powerful.

88
00:04:34,953 --> 00:04:38,071
He called it a reverse
dominance hierarchy.

89
00:04:38,091 --> 00:04:42,412
Instead of one boss ruling the
group, the group rules the boss.

90
00:04:42,432 --> 00:04:45,238
And their favourite weapon
was surprisingly gentle.

91
00:04:45,258 --> 00:04:50,730
Teasing. In the nineteen sixties, the
anthropologist Richard Lee lived with the

92
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:52,668
Ju'hoansi of the Kalahari.

93
00:04:52,688 --> 00:04:57,308
At Christmas, he bought the biggest,
fattest ox he could find, as a gift for the

94
00:04:57,328 --> 00:05:00,033
whole camp. But everyone insulted it.

95
00:05:00,053 --> 00:05:04,376
One woman asked him, do you expect
us to eat that bag of bones?

96
00:05:04,396 --> 00:05:08,113
Then the feast came, and the
ox was huge, and delicious.

97
00:05:08,133 --> 00:05:10,412
They'd been teasing him all along.

98
00:05:10,432 --> 00:05:13,245
Later, a hunter called
Tomazo explained why.

99
00:05:13,265 --> 00:05:18,219
When a young man kills much meat, he
comes to think of himself as a chief, or a

100
00:05:18,239 --> 00:05:21,819
big man, and he thinks of the
rest of us as his servants.

101
00:05:21,839 --> 00:05:23,663
We can't accept this.

102
00:05:23,683 --> 00:05:26,600
So we always speak of
his meat as worthless.

103
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:29,938
This way, we cool his
heart, and make him gentle.

104
00:05:29,958 --> 00:05:34,315
Another man, called Gaugo, explained
how a good hunter should behave.

105
00:05:34,335 --> 00:05:36,465
He mustn't come home and boast.

106
00:05:36,485 --> 00:05:41,003
He sits quietly by the fire, until
someone asks what he saw today.

107
00:05:41,023 --> 00:05:43,340
Then he says, I'm no good for hunting.

108
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:44,778
I saw nothing at all.

109
00:05:44,798 --> 00:05:46,661
Just a little tiny one.

110
00:05:46,681 --> 00:05:50,638
And everyone smiles, because they
know he's killed something big.

111
00:05:50,658 --> 00:05:51,964
And they had another trick.

112
00:05:51,984 --> 00:05:56,388
Arrows. Hunters lent and
swapped arrows all the time.

113
00:05:56,408 --> 00:06:01,138
And when an animal was killed, the meat
often belonged to the owner of the arrow,

114
00:06:01,158 --> 00:06:02,891
who might not be the hunter.

115
00:06:02,911 --> 00:06:06,791
So the credit, and the power to
share the meat, was spread around.

116
00:06:06,811 --> 00:06:10,634
In many foraging groups, food
sharing works the same way.

117
00:06:10,654 --> 00:06:11,883
You don't wait to be offered.

118
00:06:11,903 --> 00:06:14,333
You ask for your share, and you get it.

119
00:06:14,353 --> 00:06:16,684
Researchers call it demand sharing.

120
00:06:16,704 --> 00:06:17,796
So who were the police?

121
00:06:17,816 --> 00:06:22,140
Everyone. In small groups, everyone
knows everyone's business.

122
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:26,390
The psychologist Robin Dunbar's team
listened to people chatting, and found that

123
00:06:26,410 --> 00:06:30,053
about two thirds of the talk
was about social topics.

124
00:06:30,073 --> 00:06:32,865
People, relationships, who did what.

125
00:06:32,885 --> 00:06:35,306
Surprisingly little of it was nasty.

126
00:06:35,326 --> 00:06:36,781
That might sound petty.

127
00:06:36,801 --> 00:06:40,571
But gossip is a powerful
way to keep people in line.

128
00:06:40,591 --> 00:06:45,396
The anthropologist Polly Wiessner recorded
three hundred and eight Ju'hoansi

129
00:06:45,416 --> 00:06:49,888
conversations. More than half of
them included criticism of someone.

130
00:06:49,908 --> 00:06:55,396
Praise was rare. When someone acted like
a big shot, it usually started with

131
00:06:55,416 --> 00:06:56,996
jokes and teasing.

132
00:06:57,016 --> 00:07:00,265
If that didn't work, the
criticism got harsher.

133
00:07:00,285 --> 00:07:05,652
One man who kept showing off was refused
meat, and left camp for a few weeks.

134
00:07:05,672 --> 00:07:10,165
Another made peace by killing one of his
own few cows, and sharing the meat with

135
00:07:10,185 --> 00:07:13,588
everyone. The aim wasn't
to destroy someone.

136
00:07:13,608 --> 00:07:16,115
It was to bring them back into line.

137
00:07:16,135 --> 00:07:20,170
In Greenland, the Inuit had an
even more entertaining solution.

138
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:23,345
Two people with a quarrel
would hold a song duel.

139
00:07:23,365 --> 00:07:28,208
They took turns singing insulting songs
about each other, with a drum, in front

140
00:07:28,228 --> 00:07:31,101
of everyone. The audience laughed along.

141
00:07:31,121 --> 00:07:35,066
And the aim was usually to clear
the air, so life could go on.

142
00:07:35,086 --> 00:07:39,929
Though losing could be so embarrassing
that one man is said to have left and gone

143
00:07:39,949 --> 00:07:41,298
into exile.

144
00:07:41,318 --> 00:07:43,703
But what if someone was truly dangerous?

145
00:07:43,723 --> 00:07:45,683
What if teasing didn't work?

146
00:07:45,703 --> 00:07:48,821
First, people would just
stop cooperating with him.

147
00:07:48,841 --> 00:07:50,883
Not sharing. Not hunting with him.

148
00:07:50,903 --> 00:07:54,979
Not listening. And if that failed,
the group had one last option.

149
00:07:54,999 --> 00:07:57,531
Life without police
wasn't always peaceful.

150
00:07:57,551 --> 00:08:02,193
Richard Lee recorded twenty two killings
among the Ju'hoansi between nineteen

151
00:08:02,213 --> 00:08:07,118
twenty and nineteen fifty five, mostly
from fights that got out of hand.

152
00:08:07,138 --> 00:08:11,869
In one case people remembered, a man who
had killed several people was finally

153
00:08:11,889 --> 00:08:14,898
stopped by his own community, for good.

154
00:08:14,918 --> 00:08:18,648
Christopher Boehm found cases
like this in many societies.

155
00:08:18,668 --> 00:08:22,589
Rare, but real. And some
scientists think this changed us.

156
00:08:22,609 --> 00:08:27,524
The anthropologist Richard Wrangham argues
that, over many thousands of years,

157
00:08:27,544 --> 00:08:31,160
groups that stopped their worst
bullies made humans calmer.

158
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:36,097
He estimates that chimpanzees fight
within their own groups hundreds of times

159
00:08:36,117 --> 00:08:38,010
more often than people do.

160
00:08:38,030 --> 00:08:42,025
He thinks we may have, in
a sense, tamed ourselves.

161
00:08:42,045 --> 00:08:44,889
It's a hypothesis, and it's still debated.

162
00:08:44,909 --> 00:08:47,014
But it's a fascinating idea.

163
00:08:47,034 --> 00:08:51,236
Our friendliness might come from
a very old kind of justice.

164
00:08:51,256 --> 00:08:53,198
So were all hunter-gatherers equal?

165
00:08:53,218 --> 00:08:59,198
Not at all. In Florida, the Calusa lived
by fishing and gathering, not farming.

166
00:08:59,218 --> 00:09:03,461
Yet in the fifteen hundreds, their
ruler, whom the Spanish called Carlos,

167
00:09:03,481 --> 00:09:06,461
collected tribute from about fifty towns.

168
00:09:06,481 --> 00:09:11,386
In fifteen sixty six, he met the Spanish
governor of Florida, as one ruler to

169
00:09:11,406 --> 00:09:16,166
another. On the northwest coast of
North America, rich fishing peoples had

170
00:09:16,186 --> 00:09:19,641
nobles, commoners, and
even enslaved people.

171
00:09:19,661 --> 00:09:24,429
Chiefs won status by holding giant
feasts, and giving away huge amounts of

172
00:09:24,449 --> 00:09:28,832
goods. And some groups did
something even more surprising.

173
00:09:28,852 --> 00:09:31,940
They changed their
government with the seasons.

174
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:36,578
On the Great Plains of North America,
people spent much of the year in small,

175
00:09:36,598 --> 00:09:41,192
scattered bands. But in summer, they
came together for the great bison hunt.

176
00:09:41,212 --> 00:09:43,759
One wrong move could
scare the whole herd away.

177
00:09:43,779 --> 00:09:48,434
So for those weeks, some nations
appointed camp police, with real power to

178
00:09:48,454 --> 00:09:50,135
enforce the rules.

179
00:09:50,155 --> 00:09:53,620
And when the hunt was over,
that power simply ended.

180
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:58,395
Far to the north, the French scholar Marcel
Mauss noticed a similar rhythm among

181
00:09:58,415 --> 00:10:03,945
the Inuit. Small family groups in summer,
and big communal gatherings in winter,

182
00:10:03,965 --> 00:10:05,995
with different rules for each.

183
00:10:06,015 --> 00:10:09,937
In the winter houses, there was
more sharing, and more ritual.

184
00:10:09,957 --> 00:10:13,487
Some researchers argue that
this is the real story.

185
00:10:13,507 --> 00:10:17,175
For most of history, people didn't
just live without government.

186
00:10:17,195 --> 00:10:20,566
They knew how to switch it
on, and how to switch it off.

187
00:10:20,586 --> 00:10:24,073
And when they did have leaders,
those leaders had to work for it.

188
00:10:24,093 --> 00:10:29,461
The anthropologist Claude Levi-Strauss
lived with the Nambikwara in Brazil.

189
00:10:29,481 --> 00:10:32,848
Their chief couldn't force
anyone to do anything.

190
00:10:32,868 --> 00:10:37,546
He kept his followers only by being
generous, and by keeping their trust.

191
00:10:37,566 --> 00:10:42,371
The French anthropologist Pierre
Clastres argued that in many Amazonian

192
00:10:42,391 --> 00:10:48,021
societies, the chief was a peacemaker,
a giver of gifts, and a fine speaker.

193
00:10:48,041 --> 00:10:51,864
But a chief who tried to give
orders risked being deserted.

194
00:10:51,884 --> 00:10:56,772
In parts of Melanesia, big men earned
influence by gathering followers and

195
00:10:56,792 --> 00:10:59,797
throwing feasts. It wasn't inherited.

196
00:10:59,817 --> 00:11:03,983
And if a big man pushed people
too hard, they drifted away.

197
00:11:04,003 --> 00:11:05,623
So where did kings come from?

198
00:11:05,643 --> 00:11:07,818
Much of the answer begins with farming.

199
00:11:07,838 --> 00:11:09,493
Farmers could store grain.

200
00:11:09,513 --> 00:11:13,420
And stored grain can be counted,
guarded, taxed, and taken.

201
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:18,580
In twenty seventeen, researchers compared
the sizes of houses at more than sixty

202
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:23,847
ancient sites. Among hunter-gatherers,
houses were mostly about the same size.

203
00:11:23,867 --> 00:11:26,247
After farming, the differences grew.

204
00:11:26,267 --> 00:11:31,322
And in the Old World, where farmers had
oxen and horses, inequality climbed much

205
00:11:31,342 --> 00:11:37,273
higher. More than five thousand years ago,
in Mesopotamia, Uruk grew into one of

206
00:11:37,293 --> 00:11:39,111
the world's first cities.

207
00:11:39,131 --> 00:11:44,468
By about five thousand years ago, it may
have held forty thousand people or more.

208
00:11:44,488 --> 00:11:47,716
Its walls enclosed about
six square kilometres.

209
00:11:47,736 --> 00:11:50,134
Far too many people to know each other.

210
00:11:50,154 --> 00:11:52,862
And to manage it, people
invented something new.

211
00:11:52,882 --> 00:11:57,084
Writing. Much of the earliest
writing wasn't poetry or stories.

212
00:11:57,104 --> 00:12:02,002
It was accounts. Grain, sheep,
beer, rations, and work owed.

213
00:12:02,022 --> 00:12:04,626
Writing helped turn power into paperwork.

214
00:12:04,646 --> 00:12:09,589
In Egypt, royal officials counted the
cattle about every two years, to work out

215
00:12:09,609 --> 00:12:12,914
taxes. Years were even named after it.

216
00:12:12,934 --> 00:12:15,030
The year of the fifth count.

217
00:12:15,050 --> 00:12:16,394
Then came the kings.

218
00:12:16,414 --> 00:12:21,144
An ancient Sumerian list of kings
begins, after the kingship descended from

219
00:12:21,164 --> 00:12:26,169
heaven. It claims the very first king
ruled for twenty eight thousand, eight

220
00:12:26,189 --> 00:12:29,103
hundred years. And kings wrote laws.

221
00:12:29,123 --> 00:12:34,053
The oldest law code we know comes from
the city of Ur, more than four thousand

222
00:12:34,073 --> 00:12:39,371
years ago. About three centuries later,
King Hammurabi of Babylon had about two

223
00:12:39,391 --> 00:12:43,996
hundred and eighty laws carved on a
black stone pillar, more than two metres

224
00:12:44,016 --> 00:12:46,519
tall. For everyone to see.

225
00:12:46,539 --> 00:12:51,094
Centuries later, it was carried off as
war loot, and found again in Iran in

226
00:12:51,114 --> 00:12:56,091
nineteen oh one. Today, it
stands in the Louvre, in Paris.

227
00:12:56,111 --> 00:12:59,054
The Sumerians also gave us something else.

228
00:12:59,074 --> 00:13:02,192
One of the earliest
written words for freedom.

229
00:13:02,212 --> 00:13:05,269
Amargi. It literally means,
return to the mother.

230
00:13:05,289 --> 00:13:09,502
It was used when people trapped by
debts were set free to go home.

231
00:13:09,522 --> 00:13:12,790
Freedom, it seems, was
something people remembered.

232
00:13:12,810 --> 00:13:16,131
And many people did walk
away from the early states.

233
00:13:16,151 --> 00:13:21,456
The political scientist James Scott argues
that early states depended on grain,

234
00:13:21,476 --> 00:13:26,444
which is easy to see, measure, and tax,
and that escaping them was common.

235
00:13:26,464 --> 00:13:30,469
and that for thousands of years, many
people chose to live outside a state's

236
00:13:30,489 --> 00:13:31,508
reach.

237
00:13:31,528 --> 00:13:34,246
But big societies didn't
always need kings.

238
00:13:34,266 --> 00:13:39,058
About six thousand years ago, in what
is now Ukraine, people built enormous

239
00:13:39,078 --> 00:13:44,440
settlements. Some had more than a
thousand houses, laid out in great rings.

240
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:48,847
How many people lived there, and for
how much of the year, is still debated.

241
00:13:48,867 --> 00:13:51,837
But archaeologists have found no palaces.

242
00:13:51,857 --> 00:13:55,722
No royal tombs. No clear
sign of a ruling family.

243
00:13:55,742 --> 00:13:59,673
Instead, there were large buildings
that may have been meeting houses.

244
00:13:59,693 --> 00:14:05,398
In Mexico, the great city of Teotihuacan
thrived for more than six hundred years,

245
00:14:05,418 --> 00:14:08,285
from about two thousand
one hundred years ago.

246
00:14:08,305 --> 00:14:11,606
It was home to around a
hundred thousand people.

247
00:14:11,626 --> 00:14:16,343
Most of them lived in more than two
thousand apartment compounds, many with

248
00:14:16,363 --> 00:14:18,635
plastered floors and porches.

249
00:14:18,655 --> 00:14:21,637
And archaeologists have
found no royal tombs.

250
00:14:21,657 --> 00:14:24,220
How the city was ruled is still a mystery.

251
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:26,645
Kings weren't the only way to run a city.

252
00:14:26,665 --> 00:14:28,652
They were just one choice.

253
00:14:28,672 --> 00:14:32,302
For almost all of human history,
there was no government.

254
00:14:32,322 --> 00:14:35,941
Kings and states are a
very recent experiment.

255
00:14:35,961 --> 00:14:38,761
But no government never meant no rules.

256
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:41,489
The rules were enforced by everyone.

257
00:14:41,509 --> 00:14:44,586
Today, we have presidents,
parliaments, and police.

258
00:14:44,606 --> 00:14:47,086
And they do things no band ever could.

259
00:14:47,106 --> 00:14:49,773
Roads. Hospitals. Schools.

260
00:14:49,793 --> 00:14:53,079
But the oldest tools of
justice are still with us.

261
00:14:53,099 --> 00:14:55,017
Gossip. Teasing. Sharing.

262
00:14:55,037 --> 00:14:57,215
And the power to walk away.

263
00:14:57,235 --> 00:15:02,303
Fifty thousand years ago, a hunter came
home with a huge animal, and everyone

264
00:15:02,323 --> 00:15:04,353
told him it was worthless.

265
00:15:04,373 --> 00:15:08,810
Not to be cruel. But so that
no one would ever be the boss.

266
00:15:08,830 --> 00:15:10,385
You vote every few years.

267
00:15:10,405 --> 00:15:12,505
They voted every day.
